Matt and all,

First and foremost, to the new R learners out there, tidyverse or not, either is fine. As with R vs Python, etc. starting with the tools that your community uses is probably the way to go. Find a way to do what you need to do (and by all means ask questions on this listserv and at our meetings -- we want to help!), and as you keep going deeper, your toolset will naturally diversify as well.

Matt, no one is saying that data should always be in tidy data frames or will always arrive that way or that learners shouldn't learn the basics of indexing and subsetting in base R. I think it's unfair to say that learners who start with tidyverse tools are just stringing together commands to find something that appears to work. Code is opaque when you're new to it no matter what. I suspect that tidyverse functions become clearer faster to new learners, and that they are therefore more quickly able to confidently do the basic manipulation, analysis, and visualization that constitutes all of what most will ever do with R. If they then choose to go deeper, there may be some hiccups, but maybe they get there faster, maybe there's less attRition, etc. I was just talking with a fellow Software/Data Carpentry instructor about how cool it would be to design parallel workshops in base and tidyverse and see how the differ in terms of student enjoyment, immediate and long-term learning gains, continued use of R, etc. Anyone want an education research side project??

